                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Solid action platformer just like the good old days.  \n\nYou are the son of the hero who vanquished the bad guy the last time.  Now it's your turn to beat up the baddies and save the world!  \n\nYou start with a throwing dagger that can upgrade in size and power by collecting a power up.  There are also throwing axes and a blue energy spell you can find or buy from a traveling merchant.   Jump and double jump to platforms to defeat enemies and maybe find hidden things like food to increase you life or gems.  Collect a key to open doors to continue to the next section.\n\nControls feel solid.  I used the Xbox controller but you can also use keyboard.  I had no trouble with landing on platforms, which sometimes happens with indie platformers.  No slipping like the platforms are covered with ice (unless there really are platforms covered with ice in later levels, I don't know yet) and falling to your doom.  Nope, in this game when you jump, you land solid.  The only problem I ran into was enemies on platforms above you can hit you sometimes because your character jumps so high, even with single jump.  But that's just a matter of getting used to how the game works.\n\nSo far it's been fairly easy, though I am no expert.  From the title I assumed it would be hard as balls, but it's actually really fun.  I died in the first section once, then restarted and made it into the second section before dying again.  If you're a hardcore platformer you might be able to just whip right through this game in one sitting, I don't know.  I assume it gets harder as the game goes on.\n\nGraphics are nice, sort of a combination of pixels and cartoons.  You can see from the screenshots if you like them or not.\n\nSound:  The story is voiced by actors.  There was a short intro and then another short scene before the first boss.  I don't remember the music much, which just means it wasn't super good nor super bad.  \n\nOh, I found one spot where you go could in one of two directions.  You get 1 key but there are 2 doors.  So that's pretty cool.  It looks like a one-time choice,  not like it loops around.  That's nice for a bit of replayability.\n\nPrice seems right for the quality.  Though, like I said, I don't know how long the game is yet.",
